// Polkit rule letting the spoloh service account set the clock, the time
// zone and the NTP switch, and start one named unit.
//
// Install to /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/50-spoloh-timedate.rules.
//
// Why this is needed: spoloh runs as User=spoloh (see spoloh.service), and
// systemd-timedated's D-Bus API is polkit-gated. A system service has no
// active login session, so its requests fall to polkit's inactive/any
// defaults rather than the allow_active ones a desktop user gets. Without a
// rule, every timedatectl call the /time page makes is denied and the
// operator is back to an SSH session for a device that is meant to need
// none.
//
// The three timedate1 actions granted are the ones the daemon actually
// performs, and no others:
//
//   set-timezone  the zone picker, which also writes config.Device.Timezone
//   set-ntp       the synchronisation switch
//   set-time      setting the clock by hand, which systemd itself refuses
//                 while set-ntp is on
//
// Deliberately not granted: set-local-rtc. The dashboard reports LocalRTC
// and never changes it, and a service that can announce air raid alerts
// should not also be able to reinterpret the hardware clock.
//
// The fourth grant is the one to read carefully.
// org.freedesktop.systemd1.manage-units is how the daemon starts
// spoloh-timesync.service, the root oneshot that carries the configured NTP
// server into /etc/systemd/timesyncd.conf.d/ — a directory this daemon
// cannot write. Granted unconditionally, that action would let anything
// reaching the dashboard over HTTP start, stop or restart any unit on the
// board: sshd, the firewall, this daemon's own watchdog. So it is pinned to
// one unit name and one verb, through the details systemd puts on the
// action:
//
//   action.lookup("unit") === "spoloh-timesync.service"
//   action.lookup("verb") === "start"
//
// Both checks, not either. A rule that matched the unit but not the verb
// would also permit stop and restart of it, which is harmless for a oneshot
// but is not the thing that was asked for; a rule that matched neither is
// the hole described above. The unit name here and the timesyncUnit
// constant in internal/timedate/timedatectl.go have to agree — change one
// and polkit denies the other.
//
// UNVERIFIED ON HARDWARE. Neither this file nor the NetworkManager rule
// beside it has been run against a real board; nothing on a development
// machine can exercise polkit against systemd-timedated. The actions are
// read off the operations in internal/timedate/timedatectl.go and the
// shape is copied from 50-spoloh-networkmanager.rules, but treat it as a
// hypothesis until an install has confirmed it. If the /time page reports
// "Не вдалося прочитати налаштування часу" or refuses every change on a
// board that does have timedatectl, this file is the first place to look:
// check `journalctl -u polkit` for a denial naming one of the actions
// above.
//
// polkit >= 0.106 reads this JavaScript format. Distributions still
// shipping polkit 0.105 (Debian bullseye and older) ignore rules.d entirely
// and need the equivalent as a .pkla file under
// /etc/polkit-1/localauthority/50-local.d/ instead. The target board is
// Debian 13 with polkit 126, so this format is the right one for it.

polkit.addRule(function (action, subject) {
    if (subject.user !== "spoloh") {
        return polkit.Result.NOT_HANDLED;
    }
    if (action.id === "org.freedesktop.timedate1.set-timezone" ||
        action.id === "org.freedesktop.timedate1.set-ntp" ||
        action.id === "org.freedesktop.timedate1.set-time") {
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    }
    if (action.id === "org.freedesktop.systemd1.manage-units" &&
        action.lookup("unit") === "spoloh-timesync.service" &&
        action.lookup("verb") === "start") {
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    }
    return polkit.Result.NOT_HANDLED;
});
